   "slug": "companion-weather",
   "title": "Companion Weather",
   "by": "",
   "year": "2026",
   "genre": "Dream-Pop",
   "wash": "#7a4a14",
   "sc": "https://soundcloud.com/chemtrails/sets/companion-weather",
   "total": "1:27:44",
   "desc": "A record that sneaks up on you. On paper, an album built around songs about current and past pets sounds like it might drift toward sentiment, whimsy, or novelty. But this one doesn’t. Instead, it lands in a far rarer place — tender, strange, bruised, funny in spots, and quietly devastating in the way only the most personal records can be.\n\nWhat makes Companion Weather work is that it understands a truth most people don’t say out loud: animals are never just animals. They become roommates, witnesses, minor gods of the domestic world. They watch us unravel, recover, fall in love, lose people, change houses, change years, and become versions of ourselves we never meant to be. They sit in the background of our lives until one day we realize they were never in the background at all. This album knows that. Every song feels less like a sketch of a pet and more like a weather report from a life once shared.\n\nThere’s something deeply 1970s about the emotional temperature here. The record feels lived-in rather than manufactured — warm tape hiss, fading light, old furniture, worn sleeves, and that particular late-night honesty that only shows up when nobody is trying to impress anyone. It doesn’t overreach. It doesn’t beg for tears. It just tells the truth softly enough that the sadness has room to breathe. That restraint gives the whole thing weight.\n\nAnd that’s really the secret of Companion Weather: it isn’t just about cats and dogs and beloved companions, though they are its heart. It’s about memory. Routine. Grief. The shape of a house after someone — or something — is gone. The strange dignity of everyday love. The bowl in the corner. The sound in the hallway that isn’t there anymore. The way devotion can make even the smallest life feel mythic.\n\nAt its best, the album feels like a stack of old snapshots left near a turntable during a storm. It’s intimate without becoming precious, melancholic without collapsing under its own sadness, and affectionate without ever turning cute. That’s a difficult balance to strike, but Companion Weather finds it. The result is a record that feels less like a concept album and more like a real emotional climate — a little sun, a little rain, a little ghost in the room.\n\nCompanion Weather isn’t an album about pets in the simple sense. It’s about the emotional weather they leave behind — and the lives we measure, however quietly, by who once waited for us at the door.",
